'''Belone''' (bel′ọ-nē), ''n.'' [L., < Gr. βελόνη, any sharp point, a needle, < βέλος, an arrow, dart, any missile, < βάλλειν, throw.] A genus of fishes remarkable for their slender and elongated jaws, representing in some systems a family ''Belonidæ'', in others referred to the ''Scomberesocidæ;'' the garfishes.

'''belong''' (bē-lông′), ''v. i.'' [< ME. ''belongen'' (= D. ''belangen'', concern, = OHG. ''belangēn'', MHG. G. ''belangen'', reach to, attain, concern, affect; associated with the adj., early ME. ''belong'' (= OS. ''bilang'' = MD. ''belangh''), equiv. to AS. ''gelang'', ME. ''ylong'', ''ilong'', ''along'', ''long'', mod. E. ''along''<sup>2</sup>, ''long''<sup>3</sup>, belonging, along), < ''be-''<sup>1</sup> + ''longen'', belong (there is no AS. *''belangian'' or *''belang''): see ''along''<sup>2</sup>, ''long''<sup>2</sup>, ''long''<sup>3</sup>.] '''1.''' To go along with anything, or accompany it as an adjunct or attribute; pertain; appertain; be a property (of); be in the power or at the disposal (of). [In all senses except 7 followed by ''to'', or in the older English by ''unto''.]
{{smaller block|Her hap was to light on a part of the field ''belonging'' unto Boaz.
{{right|Ruth ii. 3.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
{{smaller block|And David said unto him, To whom ''belongest'' thou?
{{right|1 Sam. xxx. 13.}}
To the Lord our God ''belong'' mercies and forgivenesses.
{{right|Dan. ix. 9.}}
He . . . careth for the things that ''belong'' to the Lord.
{{right|1 Cor. vii. 32.}}
Most of the males subject to him [the father of the family] are really his children, but, even if they have not sprung from him, they are subject to him, they form part of his household, they (if a word coloured by later notions be used) ''belong'' to him.
{{right|''Maine'', Early Law and Custom, p. 87.}}}}
'''2.''' To be the concern or proper business (of); appertain (to): as, it ''belongs'' to John Doe to prove the title.
{{smaller block|<poem>To you it doth ''belong''
Yourself to pardon of self-doing crime.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, lviii.}}}}
'''3.''' To be appendant (to); be connected (with); be a special relation (to): as, a beam or rafter ''belongs'' to such a frame, or to such a place in the building.
{{smaller block|He took them, and went aside privately into a desert place ''belonging'' to the city called Bethsaida. Luke ix. 10.}}
'''4.''' To be suitable; be due.
{{smaller block|Strong meat ''belongeth'' to them that are of full age.
{{right|Heb. v. 14.}}
<poem>Hearing . . . thy beauty sounded,
(Yet not so deeply as to thee ''belongs'',)
Myself am mov’d to woo thee for my wife.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., ii. 1.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
'''5.''' To have a settled residence (in); be domiciled (in); specifically, have a legal residence, settlement, or inhabitancy (in), whether by birth or operation of law, so as to be chargeable upon the parish or town: said of a pauper, or one likely to become such.
{{smaller block|Bastards also are settled in the parishes to which the mothers ''belong''.
{{right|''Blackstone'', Com., I. xvi.}}}}

'''6.''' To be a native (of); have original residence (in).
{{smaller block|There is no other country in the world to which the gipsies could ''belong''.
{{right|''M. Raper.''}}}}

'''7.''' To have its (or one’s) proper place; be resident: as, this book ''belongs'' on the top shelf; I ''belong'' here (in this house or town). [U. S.]

'''belonging''' (bẹ̄-lông′ing), ''n.'' [< ''belong'' + ''-ing''<sup>1</sup>.] That which belongs to one: used generally, if not always, in the plural. {{smaller|(''a'') Qualities; endowments; faculties.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Thyself and thy ''belongings''
Are not thine own so proper, as to waste
Thyself upon thy virtues, they on thee.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., i. 1.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Property; possessions: as, “I carry all my ''belongings'' with me,” ''Trollope.'' (''c'') Members of one’s family or household; relations or dependants. [Humorous.]}}
{{smaller block|When Lady Kew said, “''sic volo, sic jubeo,''” I promise you few persons of her ladyship’s ''belongings'' stopped, before they did her biddings, to ask her reasons.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Newcomes, xxxiii.}}
I have been trouble enough to my ''belongings'' in my day.
{{right|''Dickens'', Bleak House, II. 103.}}}}
{{smaller|(''d'') Appendages.}}
{{smaller block|The ''belongings'' to this Indian-looking robe.
{{right|''Cornhill Mag.''}}}}

'''belonid''' (bel′ọ̄-nid), ''n.'' [< ''Belonidæ.''] A fish of the family ''Belonidæ.''

